      type: 'intro',
      title: 'The most dynamically active ice giant — and its backward moon',
      body: `Neptune is the most distant planet from the Sun, orbiting at 30.07 AU with a period of 164.8 years — so long that it has completed less than one orbit since its discovery on September 23, 1846. That discovery itself was a triumph of Newtonian mechanics: astronomers noticed that Uranus was not following its predicted orbit precisely, and mathematical analyses by Urbain Le Verrier (France) and John Couch Adams (England) independently predicted the position of an unseen eighth planet based solely on the gravitational perturbations it was exerting on Uranus. When Johann Gottfried Galle pointed a telescope to the predicted location, Neptune was found within 1° of the prediction — the first planet discovered by mathematics rather than observation.

Despite being smaller than Uranus (17.15 vs 14.5 Earth masses), Neptune is slightly denser (1.638 vs 1.27 g/cm³) and — crucially — far more energetically active. Neptune radiates approximately 2.6 times more energy than it receives from the Sun, an excess heat emission comparable to Jupiter's 1.7× ratio and dramatically larger than Uranus's near-zero excess. The reason for this contrast between two otherwise similar ice giants remains one of the most puzzling open questions in planetary science. The leading hypothesis is that Uranus's giant impact created a stably stratified interior that traps heat, while Neptune's interior lacks this suppression and convects more freely — but direct evidence from interior models remains inconclusive, and settling the question is a primary science goal for planned ice giant missions.

Neptune's atmosphere is among the most violently windy of any planet. Measured wind speeds reach up to 2,100 km/h (580 m/s) near the Great Dark Spot — the fastest winds in the Solar System. These winds blow westward, opposite to Neptune's eastward rotation, making them retrograde relative to the planet's spin — a pattern also observed on Uranus and thought to result from deep internal heat driving convection that overwhelms Solar forcing. When Voyager 2 flew past Neptune on August 25, 1989 — the only spacecraft ever to visit — it imaged a Great Dark Spot roughly the size of Earth, an anti-cyclonic storm with a brilliant white cloud feature ("Scooter") racing around it. By 1994, when Hubble Space Telescope turned its gaze to Neptune, the Great Dark Spot had vanished — confirming it is not a permanent feature like Jupiter's Great Red Spot but a transient storm. New dark spots have appeared and disappeared since, revealing a dynamic, constantly evolving atmosphere.

Neptune's most remarkable feature is not the planet itself but its largest moon, Triton. Triton is unique among large moons in the Solar System in that it orbits Neptune in a retrograde direction — opposite to Neptune's rotation. This retrograde orbit is dynamical proof that Triton is not a native moon that formed alongside Neptune but a captured object, almost certainly a Kuiper Belt Object (KBO) seized by Neptune's gravity during the early Solar System. How Neptune captured Triton is still debated; the leading mechanism involves a binary KBO system (two objects orbiting each other) making a close approach to Neptune, where one component was captured while the other was ejected. Triton's retrograde orbit creates tidal interactions with Neptune that continuously steal orbital energy — Triton is spiraling slowly inward and will cross Neptune's Roche limit in approximately 3.6 billion years, where it will be torn into a ring system grander than Saturn's current rings. Voyager 2 found that Triton is geologically active today: nitrogen geysers erupt from dark spots in its south polar cap, launched ~8 km into the thin nitrogen atmosphere before being blown downwind. Triton's surface temperature of 38 K makes it the coldest body measured by Voyager, and yet these geysers suggest something — perhaps solar warming of dark subsurface material — is driving current activity.`,
