      body: `Neptune's atmosphere is the most violently dynamic in the Solar System, a direct consequence of the planet's enormous internal heat engine. Unlike Uranus — which radiates essentially no excess heat and presents a featureless, quiescent atmosphere — Neptune radiates 2.6 times more energy than it receives from the Sun, and that internal heat drives vigorous convection from the interior upward through the atmosphere. The result is supersonic retrograde winds reaching 2,100 km/h: jet streams that blow westward, opposite to Neptune's prograde rotation, in a pattern driven by deep-interior convection rather than by solar heating. Voyager 2's 1989 flyby captured the Great Dark Spot, an Earth-sized anti-cyclonic vortex with a bright white streak called "Scooter" racing around its perimeter. By 1994, Hubble confirmed the storm had vanished entirely — replaced by new spots elsewhere on the disk. Neptune's atmosphere is not merely stormy; it is continuously generating and dissolving enormous storm systems on timescales of years to decades, a behaviour tied directly to its excess internal heat emission.

Triton's story is one of the most dramatic in Solar System science: a world born in the outer Kuiper Belt, gravitationally seized by Neptune during the dynamically chaotic era described by the Nice model, and now condemned by the physics of its own retrograde orbit. Every large moon in the Solar System except Triton orbits its planet in the prograde direction — the natural outcome of forming within the rotating disk of gas and dust that surrounded the young planet. Triton's retrograde orbit is a gravitational signature that cannot be explained by in-place formation; it requires capture. The leading mechanism is a binary exchange reaction: two Kuiper Belt Objects orbiting each other as a pair made a close approach to Neptune, and the energy of the pair's mutual orbit was donated to the capture process — one KBO was seized by Neptune (becoming Triton) while the other was flung away. The aftermath of that capture is playing out across geological time: tidal friction in a retrograde orbit continuously removes angular momentum from Triton's orbit, causing it to spiral slowly inward. In approximately 3.6 billion years it will cross the Roche limit and be shredded into a ring system that could rival or surpass Saturn's rings in mass and grandeur. In the meantime, Voyager 2 found that Triton is not passively waiting for its fate — nitrogen geysers erupting from its polar cap prove the frozen world is geologically active right now, driven by a solid-state greenhouse mechanism in which sunlight penetrates translucent nitrogen ice and vaporises subsurface material.`,
